        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What should a ribbon OEM supply agreement actually cover beyond price and quantity?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A usable ribbon supply agreement covers six commercial areas that a purchase order never does: lead time and its remedies, capacity reservation for peak periods, how price may change over the life of the agreement, ownership of artwork, tooling, samples and any custom-made equipment, quality warranty and remedies, and the limits of each party's liability. The purpose of each clause is the same: to convert a question that will otherwise be answered by whoever has more leverage at the moment, into a rule that was agreed when neither party was under pressure. In our experience the clauses that cause the most conflict are the ones buyers skip because they feel like legal formalities: the lead time remedy and the price adjustment mechanism. Those two are the ones that decide who absorbs a delay caused by a material shortage or a spike in polyester or nylon price."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "How do I write a lead time guarantee that the factory will actually honour?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A lead time clause needs four components, not one. A defined start point, because a lead time that starts when the order arrives is meaningless if the factory is waiting on yarn; use the point at which the order is confirmed against a specification and the material is available. A defined end point, ideally despatch rather than production completion. A calendar basis, because ribbon production in China has a Lunar New Year shutdown of roughly two to three weeks that must be excluded explicitly, otherwise the clause is unusable from December to March. And a remedy, because a guarantee without a remedy is a statement of intent: a fixed credit for each day late, capped at a stated percentage of the order value, is enforceable and common in practice, whereas a right to cancel applies only to the orders where delay is actually catastrophic. Write the remedy so it costs the factory less to hit the date than to miss it."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What is a capacity reservation clause and how is it different from a volume commitment?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A volume commitment is about how much you promise to buy. A capacity reservation is about how much capacity the factory promises to hold for you. They are separate obligations and they protect different things. Your volume commitment gives the factory certainty of revenue and is what justifies a price discount. Their reservation gives you certainty of a slot in a specific week, which is the only thing that actually protects a launch date. The reservation typically specifies a notice period, a minimum committed quantity for the reserved window, a deposit or advance, a deadline for confirming the balance, and a re-booking rule if the balance arrives late. The most useful practical addition is a second reservation covering the restart weeks immediately after the Lunar New Year break, because the post-holiday queue is where spring deliveries actually die."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "How should price adjustment work in a long-term ribbon supply agreement?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Use a formula, not a negotiation, and define the indices precisely. A workable mechanism names the specific raw material the product depends on, names the published index used to track it, sets a base price and the base index value at the time of signing, specifies a threshold band around which no adjustment occurs, and then adjusts by a stated proportion of the material movement beyond that band, subject to a stated annual cap. The threshold matters as much as the formula: it absorbs normal fluctuation without paperwork, and it stops a small index move becoming a price conversation every quarter. The cap protects you from an extreme spike arriving in a single invoice. The common failure is a clause with no threshold and no cap, which means every quarter becomes a price negotiation and the factory, holding the delivery relationship, effectively holds the price. If your agreement cannot carry a formula, insist on a fixed annual price with a defined annual review point instead, because a fixed price with a review is better than an open-ended escalator."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Who should own the artwork, tooling and custom equipment used to make my ribbon?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "You, and the clause should say so in that many words, with a trigger for transfer and a schedule for it. Artwork ownership should pass to you on creation or on payment, and it should expressly include the right to grant a sublicence to a competing supplier. Tooling is different from artwork: custom printing plates, dies, embroidery frames, slitting blades set for your width and any bespoke winding mandrel should be identified in a schedule, and the agreement should state whether the factory owns them and licenses their use, or whether you own them and the factory holds them as bailee. The schedule matters more than the principle, because tooling is only protectable if it is identifiable, and a general clause covering &ldquo;any tooling used in the production of the goods&rdquo; is very difficult to enforce against a factory that has quietly put a plate to work on someone else's order. Include a return obligation with a reasonable timeframe if you terminate, and specify what happens to the tooling if you leave the supplier: it stays with you, and the factory has a time-limited obligation to make it available for the last run of the programme."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "How do I limit a supplier's liability without making the agreement commercially unusable?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Do not attempt to exclude liability for everything, because a factory that has agreed unlimited liability for a defect in a polyester ribbon will price that risk into every quote and the resulting price is worse than the exposure. The workable structure is a tiered one. Commercial commitment: the factory is responsible for the goods failing to conform to the agreed specification and is required to replace, rework or credit them, which is the remedy that actually solves your commercial problem and should be your primary protection. Consequential loss: cap this at a stated multiple of the order value or a fixed amount, and exclude categories such as lost profit and production downtime, because these are the categories that make agreements unnegotiable. Third-party liability and product safety: this is the one area where you should not accept a low cap, because a ribbon used in a food or cosmetic gift set that carries a chemical compliance failure can expose you to a regulatory claim and a retailer suspension that dwarf the value of the order. Require the factory to warrant that materials comply with the chemical safety requirements applicable in the destination market, to provide test reports on request, and to maintain product liability insurance with the certificate available for inspection, while confirming whether your customer requires you to carry it as well."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What should the quality warranty clause cover and what should happen when a lot fails?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "The warranty should tie the goods to two documents: the purchase specification and the approved sample, and it should say that conformity to either is conformity to the contract. It should define the defect classes that are covered, so that width deviation, shade deviation, print registration failure, fraying, wrong core, wrong length and contamination are all named rather than left to a general standard of quality. It should state the inspection level and the acceptance standard used at despatch, which for most programmes should be ISO 2859-1 or ANSI Z1.4 general inspection level II with an agreed AQL, and it should specify that a failed lot is quarantined and not shipped until a written concession is signed. Then it needs the remedy and the clock: replacement or credit within a stated number of days of a documented rejection, inspection within a stated number of hours of arrival, and a claim window in days from arrival. Without the clock, the warranty is unenforceable in practice, because the factory will always be waiting for a resolution on a lot that has already been consumed by your production line."
            }
        },
        {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What dispute resolution and governing law terms are appropriate for a Chinese ribbon supplier?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Keep it simple and keep it enforceable where the assets are. Specify the governing law, and specify the forum, and make sure the forum is somewhere the dispute can actually be heard. For a supply agreement with a Chinese factory, the practical options are arbitration in a named institution under rules that both parties can use, or the courts of the agreed jurisdiction, and the choice is less important than two practical details: that the language of the agreement and of any evidence is unambiguous, and that there is a mechanism for interim relief if goods are held or payments are disputed. Whatever you choose, write an escalation ladder before the formal route: a named contact at each company, a defined period to resolve at management level, and only then the formal process. The great majority of supply disputes are factual disagreements about what was agreed, and they resolve quickly if the agreement contains an agreed document hierarchy, because the argument collapses to a question of which document governs. Specify that hierarchy explicitly, and note that in a dispute, correspondence and the approved sample register together with the release paperwork outrank any informal message, which is exactly the evidence set that actually settles a ribbon quality dispute."
            }
        }
    ]
